{Dutch Defense: Leningrad Variation, Warsaw Variation. A slight inaccuracy in move order — the bishop is better placed before this pawn commits.}
1. d4 f5 2. c4 Nf6 3. Nf3 g6 4. g3 d6 5. Bg2 Bg7 {Book so far. The plan is the usual one: finish development, then decide which side to open.} 6. Nc3 O-O 7. O-O c6 8. Qc2 ! Na6 {Prophylaxis. The move looks slow, and it takes away every active idea the opponent had.} (8... f4 Rb1 fxg3 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. Rb1 Nc7 10. b3 Qe8 11. Re1 h6 ?! 12. Bb2 g5 13. e3 Bd7 14. Ne2 a5 15. Nd2 b5 16. a3 Rc8 17. Bc3 Ra8 18. Bb2 Qf7 19. cxb5 Nxb5 20. Nc4 Rfb8 21. Qd3 Nd5 22. Qd2 Nbc7 23. Nxa5 f4 24. e4 f3 25. exd5 fxg2 26. Nxc6 Rxb3 27. Nc3 Re8 28. Re3 Rb7 29. Rbe1 Nxd5 30. Nxd5 Bxc6 31. Nb4 Bd7 32. d5 Bxb2 33. Qxb2 Qxd5 34. Qc3 Qf7 35. Rf3 Qg7 36. Qb3+ e6 {The rest is technique, which is another way of saying it still has to be played accurately.} 37. Qc2 1/2-1/2
